rk  naturally fits under transparency  but so do those concerns around whether  whether it is explainable fits under  this umbrella as well  in our experience many organizations are  actually very good on the  technology and the software development  but perhaps a little bit weaker on  accountability  and that raises questions around whether  that  accountability is accessible to the  public or to the subjects of their um  their kind of products and services  whether that accountability is  responsive  um but there's an actual cost of  doing things wrong and if there isn't if  there isn't a disincentive  that can be an issue and as we've said  this question of privacy the main issue  around that  with respect to machine learning and ai  is de-anonymization  so let's get started step one is to  build a map to scope out our systems  and we do that by drawing um boxes on a  piece of paper  mapping out um data sources  data flows the technology and the  algorithms we use  and that's you know very comfortable for  many of us but actually it's not  sufficient  we also need to include the development  process  in that because that can make a  significant difference  to how safe fair and ethical our systems  are  i will see more about that in a minute  and we can't stop there we also need to  include  people because data is often about  people  um the systems are often making  decisions or  their outputs are affecting people and  there are people  in your organization who are developing  these systems  are responsible and accountable for them  and they need to be part of this picture  that we draw out  so the next step is to establish impact  what is at stake when things go wrong  what what's the fallout  and surprisingly this is um we find  people don't do this it's it's amazing  because  without doing this you can't calibrate  your response  um you need to understand what is at  stake  in order for you to justify the work  that you do to prevent it going wrong  so you nee