urity it also  allows us to have discussions around  proportionality  should we do it to what extent should we  do it how much money should we spend on  it  what disruption should it be allowed to  cause  and actually as part of the healthy  debate around  what is information security some people  over the years have proposed a fourth  pillar non-repudiation  and that's positive and healthy because  what it means is  we are constantly thinking about what  that decomposition should be  and whether actually we've been missing  out some  um risks um and not meeting against them  properly  so this kind of updating and challenge  is good and we need to see that  in this practice of machine learning and  ai  so let's go back to this field of  machine learning and ai  and ask ourselves what is the overall  objective what are we trying to  maximize or preserve is it privacy or  does that belong in information security  is it transparency is that the overall  um  target should it be something like  explainability  perhaps it's accuracy because a system  that's inaccurate is  is going to be unfair to people  and this debate um is vibrant at the  moment  i wouldn't say it's settled but  consensus is starting to emerge  and we should continue doing that  actually because people  are still publishing guidance where some  of these themes are actually not  orthogonal  they're subsets of each other all those  gaps as i said earlier  in some of them transparency is missing  as a result of our own work and quite a  lot of  discussion and debate and taking part in  kind of  virtual groups and workshops where we  really bash out and  you know kick these uh these ideas to  really test them  we have our own a consensus that i think  is right  um but the debate will continue and the  and the answer that we think is right is  this  we think the overall objective is  trustworthy systems and you can disagree  with that as an overall objective  but we found this to be a really helpful  um top level objective and underneath  that  we didn't want too many um you know  pillars and  and and facets and we've managed to know  then down to  three or four fairness which is a fairly  broad term which we can talk about a  little bit more in a minute  um you know the idea that these systems  treat people  equally and don't do any harm um  transparency  because if we don't understand how those  things work if we don't know what  they're doing  um how can we really trust them um  it's hard to trust something if you  can't see it and an important part of  that is  that transparency has to be  understandable what we see has to be  understandable  accountability is really important um  we've talked about that  in terms of um being able to respond  appropriately when things go wrong being  able to  be accessible to those who are perhaps  the victims of a system gone wrong  [Music]  and privacy is an interesting one and  we originally didn't want to include it  because it really is um  you know usually sits under the umbrella  of information security  however we have found that there are you  know a  significant class of algorithms and data  processing  which de-anonymizes data in a way that's  the  subjects of that data are perhaps not  aware of or didn't consent to  so that's becomes so significant that  it's  one of the four pillars  and actually a lot of the um themes and  objectives that people  will um raise and do care about  can fit under these four pillars  so equality is really important to to to  people  um some people care a lot about  technical accuracy  whether a system um can achieve  a good predictive behavior for example  that can fit under fairness because if  it's not behaving accurately  it's not behaving fair others will  worry about broad questions around  no system should harm anyone should  respect human autonomy  these can fit under the under the  umbrella of fairness  similarly um questions around um you  know  public insight into how systems work  naturally fits under transparency  but so do those concerns around whether  whether it is explainable fits under  this umbrella as well  in our experience many organizations are  actually very good on the  technology and the software development  but perhaps a little bit weaker on  accountability  and that raises questions around whether  that  accountability is accessible to the  public or to the subjects of their um  their kind of products and services  whether that accountability is  responsive  um but there's an actual cost of  doing things wrong and if there isn't if  there isn't a disincentive  that can be an issue and as we've said  this question of privacy the main issue  around that  with respect to machine learning and ai  is de-anonymization  so let's get started step one is to  build a map to scope out our systems  and we do that by drawing um boxes on a  piece of paper  mapping out um data sources  data flows the technology and the  algorithms we use  and that's you know very comfortable for  many of us but actually it's not  sufficient  we also need to include the development  process  in that because that can make a  significant difference  to how safe fair and ethical our systems  are  i will see more about that in a minute  and we can't stop there we also need to  include  people because data is often about  people  um the systems are often making  decisions or  their outputs are affecting people and  there are people  in your organization who are developing  these systems  are responsible and accountable for them  and they need to be part of this picture  that we draw out  so the next step is to establish impact  what is at stake when things go wrong  what what's the fallout  and surprisingly this is um we find  people don't do this it's it's amazing  because  without doing this you can't calibrate  your response  um you need to understand what is at  stake  in order for you to justify the work  that you do to prevent it going wrong  so you need to understand what happens  to your subjects  if the system goes wrong and there might  be several ways in which it goes wrong  you might be recommending which choose  to buy next in which case if you get  that wrong  there's no harm to somebody's health or  or life  i mean it might be inconvenient there  might be a little bit of a financial  cost to yourselves  if you're making systems that are  medical in nature so recommending  medication or diagnosing disease  the impact of getting that wrong is very  high  and therefore that has to drive  the resources and effort into making  sure  that that doesn't happen or there is a  suitable response if it does  and actually this is something that is  overlooked  even if the output of a system  is subtly wrong and is perhaps  imperceptible or immeasurable  when it comes to one subject when scaled  out to entire communities  this the impact can be significant  so it's worth bearing in mind that  actually you should  look at the aggregate impact not just  narrowly focus on the impact of one  individual  once we've understood the impacts and we  know what ballpark we're playing in  in terms of seriousness we then  establish  what kinds of risks apply  and that's where this framework really  comes into its own  we can look at the map that we built in  the scope  and we can methodically apply those four  pillars  fairness transparency accountability  privacy to each of those domains  we can apply it to data the algorithm  the  the people involved um the communities  involved  and and that's that's an exhaustive um  and thorough process  and will take some time to do properly  so we won't do that here  but we'll look at some examples just to  illustrate the idea  so for example you might say well how  does transparency apply to  the algorithms we're using do we  understand what the algorithms are doing  are they closed proprietary software  where we don't really know what they're  doing  or is the algorithm open is the software  open source  so who's accountable for this system  that we're building  not responsible in terms of development  and building and  testing but who's who's whose neck is  online  when when things don't go right you need  to understand who that person is  and that person might not know they're  accountable do they know what they're  accountable for  do they know what to do when things go  wrong  is there a playbook do they practice  um what actions to take when things go  wrong  we don't often see that  and accountability is not just about the  person inside your organization  we need to think about the subjects the  citizens or users  can they access your organization's  processes for identifying when  something's gone wrong  to be able to complain or to alert and  then for action to be taken in response  to that  that feedback is really important and  you should really be thinking about and  testing  whether a subject can actually reach  your organization reach the right person  and have the right corrective measures  take place and perhaps compensation as  well  does your algorithm de-anonymize data as  i said earlier  privacy is sometimes seen as a concern  of information security  but we have seen actually in bringing  data together and  using interesting algorithms can expose  more about the subjects of those data  than they thought that they had given  consent to  in terms of fairness you know you might  ask a technical question such as  is the data biased is the data drifting  after it's gone live so we might have  tested our system  works really nicely it's gone live but  then over time the input data  is moving so these are the kinds of  questions that we should be  answering as a as part of this process  of applying this framework  and there are many other kinds of  examples i could go into and it wouldn't  be interesting here to  list out all the different combinations  so once we've done that once we've kind  of established some of those risks  we need to think about proportionate  controls and measures that we put in  place and we're now in a position  where we can justify them we know why  we're doing them  and here's just some examples just to  kind of illustrate the idea  it might be that the output of our  system is  so um life-changing  and and potentially important  um to an end-user to a citizen  that actually it's only right that there  is a human  in that loop so it's not an automated  process  you know a person a qualified person  uses the output of our  automated system and is there as the  person  that um um brings the information on  news to to the citizen  that can be justified now what we said  earlier around  understanding impacts you know if we're  recommending um we choose to buy next  that's not justified and you don't need  to do that  whereas if you've made a diagnosis and  the results are potentially  good or bad or unclear this is justified  and there's another scenario where a  human in the loop makes sense  so for perhaps intermediate impact  situations  you might have a machine output where  it's not certain what the  correct answer is it's not certainly a  and it's not certainly b it's something  in between we're not really sure  and and it can make sense then to flag  to a human to say look  the the algorithm isn't certain about  what the right answer is we need a human  to get involved  and that's a very good example of human  in the loop  it might be that actually you justify  shutting down your entire system  if it's producing um very widely  um out of range answers  or people are starting to complain  that that can be justified and again  your justification is based on  you know understanding the impact  in fact you know we don't often see this  but more organizations should do it  monitoring the input where actually your  input is drifting  and is nothing like the data it was  trained on or expected  and you should be setting up automated  uh sense checking of your data and if  you know if it's quality is degrading or  it's  you know variance is all over the place  you might decide to alert or stop the  system  rather than proceed with you know unsafe  data garbage and garbage out  in other situations it's really  important to be able to justify  the decisions that your system made and  that  you know and what is at stake can  justify the expense and effort  of implementing a fully auditable and  repeatable archive of your development  process  again if you're recommending shoes that  perhaps isn't justifiable  whereas if you're recommending  medication or health treatments  and you know you can see yourself ending  up in in a court or  another process where you have to be  able to justify  how your algorithm was trained on what  data  what the parameters were and why you  thought it was safe  and and to be able to prove that you  haven't tampered with  that record and that can be a an  appropriate response as well  um and and something else actually again  that we don't see enough organizations  um  think about is is address the risk of  group think or narrowthink where  you know the team is very talented but  but actually doesn't really  understand the context from which the  data came  or the context of the users and this is  why it's really important to have  diverse teams and particularly  representative teams  working in your organization because  some things just aren't apparent unless  you have that life experience  so now that we've talked a little bit  about this framework let's compare it to  those emerging frameworks coming from  those national bodies  and this is a picture taken from the you  know eu guidance  and as i said earlier it's you know 78  similar to every other  official publication and we can see  a lot of the themes are the same you can  see transparency over here  we can see technical robustness and  safety which implies testing  which implies understanding how your  systems perform under different  circumstances  we've seen here a little bit about  privacy accountability  and i've highlighted here things around  items around um  human agency and oversight um  being able to kind of understand the  societal environmental impact of our  systems when looked at like this as a  sort of a star chart  they all make sense but actually through  the framework that we're proposing  you can see why they're important you  can see that human agency and oversight  is important in certain impact scenarios  where  it is justified that there is a human in  the loop  we can also understand how you know for  life-changing decisions it's important  that the subject has  recourse to your governance to be able  to  correct an error so you can see how the  framework that we're proposing has these  objectives  in a in a in a more logical context  i talked earlier about more mature  sectors and what we can learn from them  so let's pick out a few examples of  lessons we can learn so this question of  a black box emerges a lot in discussions  around ai ethics and  you know understandability or not about  how  um and perhaps in your network or other  other models work actually  many other sectors have systems which  are not 100 deterministic  um for example medicine we don't always  know  how and why they work in some people  medicine has an effect which we can't  explain  so that doesn't mean we don't use  medicine  what we do instead is we build our  confidence through testing  we test diversely we test at scale  until we've reached a point where we can  say  this medicine works in you know  99 or 99.99 of cases  and we're happy that in a small number  of people  there will be an adverse effect but  we've also understood what those adverse  effects are  so the point here is testing and  understanding how a system behaves  can in some cases be a good response  to the black box problem  if we look at engineering civil  engineering building machines  we've been doing that for hundreds and  thousands of years we've got quite good  at  understanding how to build them and test  them  and in particular understanding their  operating characteristics  when we build machines and tools we know  we  test for their for the situations in  which they work best  you know which temperature range they  work best in and then how do they start  to fail  outside that central region you know as  we increase the temperature or the  humidity  does material rapidly crumble or does it  take a while to break this this  development of operating characteristics  is something we need to do more of  in in the machine learning world and  testing and continuous testing  um we are actually quite good at in in  software engineering  but actually in the machine learning  community  we've too often built systems that we've  tested and then deployed  and not continuously tested them  so let's look at this question of ethics  which actually is quite  challenging for um for many people in in  a technical field  because it's very um in concrete it's  it's very kind of you know abstract and  philosophical and  hard to pin down um by its nature you  know  if you have discussions with  philosophers and ethicists  and sociologists sort of terminology  around  you know utilitarianism and  existentialism and deontological ethics  and so on and so on  and it can feel a little bit despairing  about you know how do we turn  that very lively and sometimes  wishy-washy discussion  into something concrete and practical  and  our conclusion really is  that yes ethics can be subjective and  yes there are many views on it  but ultimately um  it's a choice a set of choices that you  or your organization makes  and understanding those choices being  conscious of them  is important so  no organization is objective or neutral  in every organization is  made up of people with their own  experiences and biases and beliefs  and so on and that is you know seeps  into the technology that we build  if we're aware of that to some extent we  can try and mitigate it  but a better position this is what we  recommend organizations to do  is to actually be very public and open  about the choices you've made  particularly those which are in a gray  area  do we use postcodes or gender as part of  our algorithm  were they perhaps you know even if  they're not illegal because they're  protected characteristics  there might be a proxy for  discrimination or  or kind of treating people unfairly and  so being public and open  is the is is the best approach we  believe because  it allows people to trust what you're  doing to understand  and also to provide feedback to you to  say look we don't agree  and that's where you match you start to  kind of  match your choices your position your  your ethical position with with where  your  public or your customers are and  a bit of a motto for us is the design ai  for the society we want to be  not the society we were in the past  because too often our machine learning  systems  are learning to replicate previous  biases  and prejudices thank you very much  i hope you enjoyed that i hope it was  [Music]  useful
 

